WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.960
Now the links function is very simple.

00:05.960 --> 00:11.480
It's going to go and count how many characters you have in one value, so you are calculating the length

00:11.480 --> 00:12.280
of a value.

00:12.320 --> 00:14.120
Let's have a very simple example to understand it.

00:14.160 --> 00:16.320
Okay, so now let's say that we have the value Maria.

00:16.320 --> 00:20.680
If you apply the links function for that, what can happen is you're going to go and start counting

00:20.680 --> 00:23.120
how many characters we have inside this value.

00:23.120 --> 00:26.200
So the M is one a, 2345.

00:26.240 --> 00:28.600
In the output you will get the number five.

00:28.640 --> 00:32.480
So five is the length or the total number of characters in this value.

00:32.480 --> 00:35.000
Now let's say that you have a number like 350.

00:35.040 --> 00:38.200
If you go and apply the links function, still it's going to go and count.

00:38.240 --> 00:39.720
How many digits do we have.

00:39.760 --> 00:41.840
The three is one, five, two, three.

00:41.840 --> 00:43.920
So the total links for that is going to be three.

00:43.920 --> 00:45.680
So you can apply it even for numbers.

00:45.680 --> 00:48.520
And not only that you can go and apply it on a date value.

00:48.520 --> 00:52.400
So let's say that you have the following date 2026 first 23.

00:52.440 --> 00:57.760
So it's going to go and count each digit, each character, even the underscores not only the numbers

00:57.800 --> 00:59.560
underscore is as well a digit.

00:59.560 --> 00:59.880
Right.

00:59.880 --> 01:02.560
So the total length of this date is going to be ten.

01:02.560 --> 01:05.160
So you can apply any data type to the links function.

01:05.160 --> 01:07.720
And in the output you will get always a number.

01:07.720 --> 01:08.200
That's it.

01:08.200 --> 01:11.440
This is how you can count the number of characters in any value.

01:11.440 --> 01:13.880
Let's go back to SQL in order to practice that okay.

01:13.920 --> 01:17.960
So now we have the task calculate the length of each customer's first name.

01:18.000 --> 01:19.680
So it is very simple.

01:19.680 --> 01:24.680
We're going to go and apply the function links Len to the column first name.

01:24.720 --> 01:27.240
And we're going to call it length name.

01:27.240 --> 01:29.080
So let's go and execute it.

01:29.080 --> 01:32.120
And with that as you can see we are getting the output numbers.

01:32.120 --> 01:37.040
And these numbers are the number of characters of each name of our customers.

01:37.040 --> 01:38.760
So this is how we calculate the length.

01:38.760 --> 01:40.200
And that's it for this group.

01:40.200 --> 01:41.320
Now moving on to the next one.

01:41.320 --> 01:42.560
It's going to be very interesting.

01:42.560 --> 01:46.160
Now we're going to talk about how to extract something from a string value.

01:46.160 --> 01:49.280
And here we're going to cover now two functions the left and the right.
